<stringmatch name="ARMSHIFTGUARD #1: a count masked below 32 prints the plain shift under every value but always" min="1" max="1">return 1 &lt;&lt; \(pin &amp; 0x1f\);</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ARMSHIFTGUARD #2: a four-bit field count prints the plain shift by default" min="1" max="1">return value &lt;&lt; \(\(ctrl &amp; 0xfff\) &gt;&gt; 8\);</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ARMSHIFTGUARD #3: the 64-bit shifts keep four guards by default, needed guards the unbounded count, and always guards all three 32-bit shifts" min="8" max="8">&lt; 32u \?</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ARMSHIFTGUARD #4: by default and under never the unbounded 32-bit count prints the plain shift" min="1" max="1">return value &lt;&lt; \(count &amp; 0xff\);</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ARMSHIFTGUARD #5: always restores the guard on the masked count" min="1" max="1">\(uint4\)\(pin &amp; 0x1f\) &lt; 32u \?</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ARMSHIFTGUARD #6: with armconstshift on, a dropped guard keeps the unsigned shift of the signed literal" min="1" max="1">return \(int4\)\(\(uint4\)1 &lt;&lt; \(pin &amp; 0x1f\)\);</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ARMSHIFTGUARD #7: by default the 64-bit right shift guards the high word's shift by 32 - n" min="1" max="1">\(uint4\)\(0x20 - count &amp; 0xff\) &lt; 32u \?</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ARMSHIFTGUARD #8: never prints the complement count as the plain C shift" min="1" max="1">v2 &lt;&lt; \(0x20 - count &amp; 0xff\);</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ARMSHIFTGUARD #9: by default the 64-bit left shift also guards both word shifts by the program's count, as needed and always guard AnyShift" min="4" max="4">\(uint4\)\(count &amp; 0xff\) &lt; 32u \?</stringmatch>
